<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso IV of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1299–1336</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso the Benign, Alfonso the Kind</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso IV, 'the Benign', was king of Aragon from 1327 to 1336. As heir, in 1323–1324 he led the expedition that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Villa di Chiesa</a> (Iglesias) and winning at Lucocisterna, which made the Kingdom of Sardinia a reality within the Crown of Aragon. As king he faced war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> over Sardinia and quarrels over the inheritance of his sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-iv-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Alfonso IV of Aragon" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphonse IV (1299-1336 ; roi d'Aragon). Autorité émettrice de monnaie Barcelone (Espagne ; atelier monétaire). Atelie... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Espagne,_Catalogne,_Alphonse_III,_gros,_Barcelone_-_btv1b11335431k_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
